title: Scaling Out
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

Say the farm has 200 yards of wool. Then the design uses 200 yards.

That is the whole method. [[Common Object Studio]] sizes the [[Oka Chair]] production to what its suppliers can make without changing how they farm. When the wool runs out, the chairs stop. Now, how long that works, or if that can really be sustained long term, that's a work in progress.

A company that wants more chairs than that has two choices. It can push the farm to grow, or it can find a second farm somewhere else. The second choice is “scaling out.” The next Oka Chair is planned for Michigan, with alpaca in place of sheep's wool and whatever timber falls there instead. Same idea, different chair. Or at least different [[Materialshed]].

The [[permacomputing|Permacomputing]] people got here first: [[Scalability downwards is more significant]].

!! Has it run out yet?

No. Justin Beitzel says only 8 Oka Chairs have been made, which doesn't scratch the surface of what Baltimore and [[Willow Creek Farm]] could supply. The wool has not run short, so nobody has had to wait.

Planning for it anyway, the answer is more farms, not bigger ones. A state of Michigan grant is paying to test wool from farms there. About five are lined up as suppliers, and one is washing and processing the wool. That supply chain serves the chair and also a new acoustics product. The same kind of chain is being set up for someone in Toronto who wants to make and sell Okaterra.

Each hub is a small factory with its own supply chain. His test for "enough" is when the impact tips from regenerative to degrading, and he says that is a long way off. It assumes these chairs replace fast furniture, and don't pile on top of the consumption already there.

//Source: correspondence with Justin Beitzel, October 2026.//

